\pard\s1 5\tab Results\par

\pard\s0 The primary evaluation compared the fine-tuned BERT classifier, the prompted Gemini large language model, and Gemini with retrieval-augmented generation (LLM+RAG) on the same 99 frozen, family-disjoint test documents. Each document was evaluated under ten registered text conditions, producing 990 predictions per method and 2,970 evaluation cases in total. Macro F1 was the primary metric because the four agencies were unevenly represented and the IRS test subset contained only four documents. Accuracy, macro precision, macro recall, errors, latency, and API usage were used as supporting measures. The family-aware study is reported as the primary result; the earlier 151-document experiment is included only as historical context because it used a different test population and did not enforce family-disjoint splitting.\par

\pard\s2 5.1\tab BERT Training and Checkpoint Selection\par

\pard\s0 BERT learned the four-agency classification task rapidly. Validation macro F1 increased from 0.9907 after the first epoch to 1.0000 after the second epoch. Although the same maximum score was also observed at epochs 3, 4, and 6, epoch 2 was retained under the predefined tie rule selecting the earliest checkpoint with the highest validation macro F1. Epoch 2 also had the lowest validation loss, 0.012386. All six planned epochs were completed; the later validation fluctuation, particularly the reduction in macro F1 at epoch 5, supports the decision to select the earlier checkpoint instead of the final epoch.\par

\pard\s4 Table 3. Family-aware BERT training and validation history.\par
