\pard\s2 5.4\tab Error Analysis and Long-Input Behavior\par

\pard\s0 The BERT errors were concentrated in the conditions that removed or limited available evidence rather than in complete unmasked documents. The saved error artifacts include an IRS W-4V document classified as SSA from its unmasked beginning, an SSA-3373-BK document classified as DMV from its ending, and an SSA-1694 document classified as IRS under complete-masked and beginning-only-masked inputs. These examples suggest that short sections containing generic administrative language may not preserve enough agency-specific context for the task-specific encoder. Because only four IRS documents were present in the test partition, a single IRS error also had a comparatively large effect on macro F1.\par

\pard\s0 The plain LLM and LLM+RAG pipelines each truncated 242 inputs that exceeded the 6,000-character limit, and every truncated case in this evaluation was still classified correctly. This observation indicates that the retained beginning text was sufficient for these documents, but it does not establish that truncation is harmless for other datasets or document types. No invalid outputs, failed responses, or retries were recorded for either API-based method.\par

\pard\s2 5.5\tab RAG Contribution, Runtime, and Cost\par

\pard\s0 Gemini and Gemini+RAG generated identical predictions for all 990 document-condition pairs. RAG therefore changed zero predictions and produced no measurable classification improvement in this evaluation. Because both approaches reached the performance ceiling, this finding does not show that retrieval is generally ineffective; it shows only that retrieved context was unnecessary for these four-agency test cases. The training-only RAG stores contained 4,300 unmasked and 4,300 masked chunks. Validation diagnostics showed that retrieval diversification reduced the mean share of results contributed by the most dominant parent document from 52.7% to 38.4%, and provenance checks confirmed that masked and unmasked conditions used their intended indexes.\par

\pard\s0 The additional retrieval context increased prompt-token use, latency, and estimated cost without altering classification. Plain Gemini averaged 1,648 ms per case, compared with 1,783 ms for Gemini+RAG. RAG used more than four times as many prompt tokens and had a higher estimated generation cost. Query embeddings added an estimated $0.1127, bringing the combined estimated cost of the two API experiments to approximately $0.6893. These values are analytical estimates from the saved artifacts and may not equal final billed charges.\par

