\pard\s2 5.6\tab Historical Context and Application Experience\par

\pard\s0 In the historical 151-document experiment, BERT achieved 0.9934 accuracy and 0.9741 macro F1, while plain Gemini and Gemini+RAG each achieved 0.9868 accuracy and 0.9694 macro F1. Mean latency was 16.4 ms for BERT, 1,507.9 ms for Gemini, and 1,555.8 ms for Gemini+RAG. These scores should not be interpreted as showing improvement or decline relative to the family-aware experiment. The historical and primary experiments used different test documents and different leakage controls; a subsequent audit found that 62 of 90 non-singleton document families in the historical data crossed partition boundaries, compared with zero family overlap in the family-aware split.\par

\pard\s0 The application demonstrates the results at three levels. The family-aware whole-test page summarizes the frozen 99-document evaluation, and the family-aware single-document page replays saved predictions across the ten conditions without making new Gemini or embedding calls. The earlier interactive prototype demonstrates the end-to-end concept by classifying an input, routing it to an agency-specific preliminary agent, and returning a short guidance response. The guidance response was not formally evaluated for factual correctness, safety, or usefulness, so the application evidence supports the feasibility of routing but not the quality of downstream advice. The approximately 1.6- to 1.8-second API latency is noticeable but compatible with a conversational prototype; the displayed frozen-result pages do not measure live inference delay.\par

\pard\s2 5.7\tab Interpretation and Limitations\par

\pard\s0 The project goal was to identify a reliable first-stage router for the NewStart AI minimum viable product. All three methods satisfied this goal for complete documents, while the robustness evaluation favored the plain LLM and LLM+RAG over BERT when identifiers or document regions were removed. Between the two LLM approaches, plain Gemini provided the stronger observed operational tradeoff because it matched RAG's perfect classification performance with lower latency, token use, and estimated cost. BERT nevertheless remains relevant when local deployment, privacy, predictable infrastructure cost, or faster routing is prioritized.\par

\pard\s0 Several limitations restrict the conclusions. The primary results come from one frozen test partition of 99 documents, with only four IRS documents. The evaluation covered documents collected from four agencies and did not formally test unsupported inputs, uncertain cases, user questions, scanned images, OCR errors, or naturally noisy uploads. Perfect LLM scores therefore represent ceiling performance on this dataset rather than general evidence of universal robustness. The same documents were reused across ten transformed conditions, so the 990 cases per method are not 990 independent documents. Data review also identified quality issues, including a placeholder-like extraction and an agency-label mismatch, showing that automated extraction and metadata checks cannot replace substantive document review. Finally, the saved LLM and RAG artifacts support the reported results, but the repository does not contain a committed notebook caller for the underlying run_plain_llm_case and run_llm_rag_case functions; adding a single versioned orchestration entry point would improve reproducibility.\par

\pard\s1 6\tab Conclusion\par

\pard\s0 NewStart AI investigated whether government documents could be classified and routed among USCIS, the California DMV, SSA, and the IRS using a fine-tuned BERT classifier, a prompted Gemini LLM, and Gemini with retrieval-augmented generation. The primary family-aware design reduced leakage risk by keeping related document families in separate partitions and evaluated the same 99 frozen test documents under ten complete, partial, masked, and unmasked text conditions. All three methods achieved 1.0000 accuracy and macro F1 on complete unmasked documents, demonstrating that each approach could support the MVP's initial routing function under the primary condition.\par

\pard\s0 The most significant result was the difference in robustness and efficiency beyond the primary condition. Plain Gemini and Gemini+RAG retained a macro F1 of 1.0000 across all ten conditions, while BERT was perfect in five conditions and reached a minimum macro F1 of 0.9587. RAG changed none of Gemini's 990 predictions but increased mean latency, prompt-token use, and estimated cost. Consequently, plain Gemini was the strongest observed choice when robustness to partial or masked text was prioritized, whereas BERT remained the more attractive foundation for a local, privacy-oriented, and low-latency deployment. The unexpectedly strong LLM performance despite input truncation, BERT's rapid attainment of perfect validation macro F1 at epoch 2, and the absence of any classification benefit from RAG were the principal unexpected findings.\par

\pard\s0 Future work should broaden the dataset to additional agencies, add more IRS examples, and evaluate documents from sources and time periods not represented in the current collection. The router should be tested on unsupported and ambiguous inputs and should include confidence calibration or an abstention mechanism before it is relied upon in practice. Evaluation should also include scanned documents, OCR noise, multilingual content, partial user uploads, and questions rather than only extracted form text. For the broader agentic platform, domain experts should assess the factual accuracy, safety, and usefulness of generated guidance, with privacy protections, audit logging, monitoring, and clear boundaries against legal or benefits advice. Productionization would also require a committed end-to-end evaluation entry point, live latency and load testing, model and prompt versioning, failure handling for external APIs, and ongoing review for agency-form changes.\par

